So does that mean we should sin to show that glory revealed in His forgiveness? Does the Gospel of Grace mean a license to sin? Anticipating this argument, (or responding to scoffers amongst the unbelieving Jews), Paul writes;

*But if our unrighteousness commend the righteousness of God, what shall we say? Is God unrighteous who taketh vengeance? (I speak as a man), God forbid: for then how shall God judge the world? For if the truth of God hath more abounded through my lie unto his glory; why yet am I also judged as a sinner? And not rather, (as we be slanderously reported, and as some affirm that we say,) Let us do evil, that good may come? whose damnation is just.*

This is a side argument, so I don't want to be bogged down in it, but it further differentiates the believing from the unbelieving Jews, and their view of salvation, which they are insisting comes from works or the law generally, and specifically circumcision. A view unregenerate "Christians" still hold today, who slanderously accuse us of offering a license to sin, calling it "cheap grace," while we know it was paid for by the precious blood of Christ, whereas they offer filthy rags of their own righteousness as atonement, an insult to God. The error of Cain.

**1 Peter 2**
*9 But ye are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people; that ye should shew forth the praises of him who hath called you out of darkness into his marvellous light;

10 Which in time past were not a people, but are now the people of God: which had not obtained mercy, but now have obtained mercy.*
